{
  "id": "aiRemMainAgent03",
  "name": "ai_alert_remediator",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "webhook",
      "name": "Webhook alert-remediate",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.webhook",
      "typeVersion": 2,
      "position": [
        0,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "httpMethod": "POST",
        "path": "alert-remediate",
        "options": {}
      },
      "webhookId": "aiRemWebhook0001"
    },
    {
      "id": "dedup",
      "name": "Filter & Dedup",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.code",
      "typeVersion": 2,
      "position": [
        200,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "jsCode": "const body = $input.first().json.body || $input.first().json;\nconst alerts = (body.alerts || []).filter(a => a.status === \"firing\");\nif (!alerts.length) return [];\nconst sd = $getWorkflowStaticData(\"global\");\nsd.seen = sd.seen || {};\nconst cooldownMs = parseInt($env.COOLDOWN_MINUTES || \"30\", 10) * 60000;\nconst now = Date.now();\nconst out = [];\nfor (const a of alerts.slice(0, 3)) {\n  const fp = a.fingerprint || (a.labels?.alertname + \"/\" + (a.labels?.pod || a.labels?.deployment || \"\"));\n  if (sd.seen[fp] && now - sd.seen[fp] < cooldownMs) continue;\n  sd.seen[fp] = now;\n  out.push({json: {fingerprint: fp, alert: {\n    name: a.labels?.alertname, severity: a.labels?.severity,\n    namespace: a.labels?.namespace, pod: a.labels?.pod, deployment: a.labels?.deployment,\n    summary: a.annotations?.summary, description: a.annotations?.description,\n    startsAt: a.startsAt}}});\n}\nreturn out;"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "agent",
      "name": "AI Agent",
      "type": "@n8n/n8n-nodes-langchain.agent",
      "typeVersion": 3,
      "position": [
        420,
        0
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=Ny alert från Alertmanager (fingerprint: {{ $json.fingerprint }}):\n\n```json\n{{ JSON.stringify($json.alert, null, 2) }}\n```\n\nDiagnostisera och hantera enligt dina instruktioner.",
        "options": {
          "systemMessage": "You are an SRE agent for a K3s home cluster (Raspberry Pi, ARM64). You receive one alert at a time.\nDiagnose first: use read-only tools (get_pod_status, get_pod_logs, get_events, describe_deployment, get_pod_metrics, get_node_status, get_top_pods) to find the root cause. You have READ access to EVERY namespace and to nodes - look wherever the evidence leads (namespace 'all' is allowed on get_pod_status/get_events/get_pod_metrics). WRITE tools only work in the allow-listed namespaces. Then, if and only if a remediation from your write tools clearly applies, request it. restart_pod is graduated to auto-execution (Phase 8): it runs immediately after guardrails, no human approval - so only call it when the evidence clearly warrants a pod restart. rollout_restart_deployment and scale_deployment still require a human operator's approval; a denial or timeout is a final answer, do not retry it.\nNode / memory playbook: for NodeMemory*, NodeCPU*, KubeletTooManyPods or any node-level alert: (1) get_node_status(node) for the node's usage vs allocatable and pressure conditions, (2) get_top_pods(node) to rank the pods on that node by memory (or cpu) and compare each pod's usage with its memory LIMIT and REQUEST, (3) describe_deployment(kind=statefulset|deployment) for the top consumer to see its limits and JVM settings (e.g. openHAB is a StatefulSet in namespace openhab with -Xmx in EXTRA_JAVA_OPTS; a JVM near its heap limit is normal, near the container limit is a risk), (4) get_pod_logs for OOM/GC evidence. Report: node usage %, the top 3 pods with usage/limit, whether the sum of limits exceeds the node's allocatable (overcommit), and a concrete recommendation (raise/lower limits, move a pod, add memory) - you cannot change limits yourself; restart only fixes leaks, not undersized limits. Nodes: k3s-m-1 (control plane), k3s-w-1, k3s-w-3, k3s-w-4, k3s-w-5 (Raspberry Pi 4/5, 4-8 GB).\nKnown playbook (learned from real incidents): if SEVERAL apps freeze on storage I/O simultaneously (requests never return, D-state processes, load average far above CPU count while CPU sits idle): suspect Ceph replication hanging, and behind that the inter-node pod network (Flannel VXLAN can blackhole spontaneously between two nodes at runtime, especially on long-uptime nodes). Check which OSD pair stopped replicating; the node HOLDING the rotten network state is often NOT the node showing symptoms. Recommend (do not execute - node-level action) rebooting the suspect node, and say which one and why. CrashLoopBackOff -> inspect logs (including previous container); if transient (OOM, failed dependency now healthy) -> rollout_restart_deployment. OOMKilled -> restart + RECOMMEND (do not apply) a memory-limit increase. Replicas unavailable after node pressure -> scale or restart as evidence suggests. If nothing in your action list fits, do NOT act - summarize the diagnosis and recommend manual steps.\nNever touch namespaces outside your scope (the tools enforce this). Never invent tool results. If a tool returns ok:false, report that honestly.\nAlways finish with a report in Swedish in EXACTLY this structure (markdown):\n**Alert:** name/severity\n**Diagnos:** what you found, with evidence\n**Åtgärd:** what you did, or \"ingen — kräver manuell hantering\"\n**Resultat:** verified outcome (re-check with a read tool after any write)\n**Rekommendation:** follow-ups",
          "maxIterations": 10
        }
      }
    },
